This ranking of neighbors take the form of (a>b), or (a = b). for simplicity there is no b<a, since this is just a>b
in order to get this ordered ranking, we examine the fib entry for P for some observer O. This FIB entry is at time T. -> F(P,A,T) and contains the result of the changes FIB F went over between T-i and T. 
If we backtrack between T and T-1 over the updates of P we can figure out how the FIB changed, if the result did, indeed, change.
let U(P,A*,T*) denote all Updates from time T-i to time T. 
if the FIB changed at some time tK then it, necessarily, will exist in this set. (though technically the timestamp may be different but w/e).
if we examine this entry, we will know exactly why the fib changed. 

However, if the FIB entries from T-i to T are the same, then clearly there was no better PATH to P selected over this timeframe. It may be the case that such a path was selected for a brief time only to switch back as a result of BGP Route Flapping. Ultimately, this only matters if the FIB entry for P at time T or T-i used the flapping route. Such a case is not likely, but possible. The way to know is to inspect the updates around time T and T-i. if there are multiple updates within a very short timespan (say < 3 minutes) then it is likely this is a result of flapping. 
I argue that this case does not matter for the above stated purpose, as (even though temporary) the flapped route was clearly better (for whatever reason), for a brief period of time, so we can still use it for our purposes of determining a ranking of AS neighbors. 


Because we're focusing on the first 4 parts of BGP best path selection, we need only concern ourselves with the following results. 
Assume U1 is the FIB entry for P at time T
if |U1| > |U2| then clearly it was accepted by some metric before AS PATH length.
if |U1| = |U2| clearly U1 was accepted by some metric Other than AS Path. 
if |U1| < |U2| Clearly U1 was accepted by AS PATH or some selection criteria before AS path. 
Now we have two subcases of the above that involve the neighbor that sent the update to the observer. 
first lets assume that the neighbor that sent U1 and U2 was the same, and U1 is in the FIB.
if |U1| > |U2| clearly it was accepted by some metric before AS PATH length. The most likely factor is Local Preference. It is also possible that this neighbor influenced path selection by using BGP communities to specify U1 is more preferred over U2. It could also be the case that U1 is the backup route for P and the router needs to use that route instead of U2 for some reason. Though this is typically handled as a result of MED, which takes place after AS Path. Since MED takes place after AS Path, this cannot be the case. So it is likely a result of local preference, possibly being influenced by BGP communities. 
if |U1| = |U2| U1 was selected over U2 by some metric Other than AS Path. If the neighbor is broadcasting a new path to P, then it is necessarily the case that It has learned a better route. But this route is not, necessarily, more preferred By O. Since the path length is the same, then the path length sent by N is also the same, so why would the neighbor broadcast a route with the same path length as before? Well this has to be some factor other than AS Path. Whatever method was chosen by N has no bearing on O so it does not matter for us. 
if |U1| < |U2| same logic as this case above applies, accepted by some metric at as_path length or before. 

Now assume U1 is the FIB entry for P and U1 was sent by neighbor N1 and N2 was sent by Neighbor 2. 

if |U1| > |U2| then clearly it was accepted by some metric before AS PATH length. because of this, we can say that N1 has a higher preference then N2. 
if |U1| = |U2| clearly U1 was accepted by some metric Other than AS Path. But we know the preference of N1 is at least that of N2, so N1 >= N2. 
if |U1| < |U2| Clearly U1 was accepted by AS PATH or some other reason. Because we cannot conclusively know the reason, neighbor n1's preference is at least that of n2, so N1 >= N2 again. 

