Fundementally, this brings up an issue that is solvable: is the FIB entry for P 'stable'. In your example above, if the FIB were to adopt A(n_2, t_3), this would be an example of an unstable FIB entry. 
So how do we know whether or not a given Fib entry is stable or not? I believe the answer is BGP Path Convergence. 
Simply scan the updates in reverse chronological order for any W(p,t_w), where p is the prefix we are currently training for. If we discover W(p,t_w) where t_w is +- 3 minutes from our fib entry time t, we cannot use this FIB entry for training. 

