let F(p,t,n_f) denote the observed FIB entry for prefix p at time t. note that n_f is the neighbor that sent the Update U_f(p,t_i,n_f) that was adopted. It must be the case that this update exists, otherwise F(p,t,n_f) would not exist. One small note: in public BGP RIB data, from which we derive the FIB for an observer, the timestamp of the route is given as the timestamp of the requested RIB, in this case time t. Because of this, we need to find the update that was adopted into the fib. 

Assume there exists U_f(p,t_i,n_f) = F(p,t,n_f).
We want to find U_f(p,t_i,n_f) such that U_f=F from a set of Updates, so we know exactly what other updates we can compare against to determine local preference (LP) and hot_potato preference (HPP) (more on HPP later).
More formally,
let Updates_P(t_-1, t) denote the set of all updates that contain prefix P from time t_-1 to t. 
We want to find U_f(p,t_i,n_f) \in Updates_P(t_-1, t) where U_f(p,t_i,n_f) = F(p,t,n_f).
It may be the case that there are many such U_f(p,t_i,n_f) \in Updates_P(t_-1, t) with different t_i. In order to be certain which t_i is correct, we will need to use the latest t_i. To be more specific, we need to use the t_i with the greatest unix timestamp value. Why should we pick this t_i and not some other t_i?
Consider the updates from t_i+j to t. We know the update at time t_i was adopted, and the updates from t_i+j to t were not. Since none of these updates were adopted, we know that these updates are less prefereable than the update at t_i. 

